    {
      "latin": "ūncia, ūnciae f.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "one twelfth",
      "evidence": [
        "Cic. Att. 13.48.1: <span lang=\"la\">Caesar, opīnor, ex ūnciā</span>",
        "Hor. Ars 327",
        "Traupman 355"
      ],
      "comment": "The Romans divided the <span lang=\"la\">as</span>, and anything else they counted in twelfths (an inheritance, a pound, a foot, an acre, interest, the hour), by these names. Each larger name counts <span lang=\"la\">ūnciae</span>: <span lang=\"la\">quīncūnx</span> and <span lang=\"la\">septūnx</span> are “five” and “seven ounces”, <span lang=\"la\">dōdrāns</span>, <span lang=\"la\">dēxtāns</span> and <span lang=\"la\">deūnx</span> are the <span lang=\"la\">as</span> less a quarter, a sixth and an ounce. On the bronze coins of the Republic the <span lang=\"la\">sēmis</span> is marked S and each <span lang=\"la\">ūncia</span> a pellet. On the clock each twelfth of the hour is five minutes: see “telling the time”.",
      "notes": [
        "uncia"
      ]
    },
